So the chain should only run when v > 1: for i := v-1; i >= 0; i-- — for v=1, i starts at 0 and generates the fly line. Therefore the loop should be `for i := v - 1; i > 0; i--` — no wait, for verse 2 (spider), the expected chain is "She swallowed the spider to catch the fly." — i=1 (spider) with the catch target being fly. So the loop is: for i := v-1; i >= 0; i--, but skip when i == v-1 and v == 1? Actually, for verse 2, the chain starts from the current animal (spider, i=1). For verse 1, there's no chain. So the loop is: for i := v - 1; i >= 0; i-- — for v=1, i=0 → "She swallowed the fly to catch the fly." is wrong.

Hmm, but for verse 2, i=1 → "She swallowed the spider to catch the fly." is correct. So the chain includes the current animal except for verse 1. So: for i := v - 1; i >= 0; i-- with an exception: if v == 1, skip. Or: for i := v - 1; i >= 0; i-- { if i == 0 && v == 1 { continue } ... }. More simply: `for i := v - 1; i >= 0; i--` but with the condition that the chain is only added when v >= 2.

